  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe Nor'easter continues to spin well off the coast with its outer \nbands of rain extending into northeast PA and central NY. Periods of \noff-and-on rain showers are expected this afternoon and evening, \nespecially for areas east of I-81 in NY and most of northeast PA. \nAlong with rain showers, winds will become more gusty beginning this \nafternoon. Over the next 24 hours, this storm system will inch \ncloser to the coast and briefly move inland on Sunday, somewhere \nover Long Island and New Jersey. As it does, rain will become more \nwidespread and the strongest winds will occur late tonight and into \nSunday. Conditions will improve throughout the day on Monday as the \nlow moves up the the coast. Winds become calm and showers come to an \nend Monday night. \n\nWith a tight pressure gradient around the low, strong 850mb \nflow above with potential for mixing, and some higher gusts already \nobserved south of the region, sustained winds and gusts were both \nincreased. Winds were a blend of the NBM and NBM 90th percentile \nwhile the gusts are NBM 90th percentile. Even with these \nadjustments, wind advisory criteria was not met. While there is \npotential for advisory-level gusts in higher elevations based on \nHREF probabilities, confidence was too low for an advisory at any \ngiven forecast zone. The low begins to weaken Sunday night into \nMonday but breezy conditions may persist until the moves away from \nthe region Monday night.\n\nAs showers move west, they are having to overcome drier air \ninitially. In general, rain showers will be light though some brief \nperiods of moderate rainfall will also be possible, especially as \nPWATs increases with the system moving closer to shore. This update \nused a blend of guidance for QPF amounts as the latest NBM was \nslightly lower than before despite some guidance showing higher \ntotals along eastern areas of our CWA. Solutions from the RFCs and \nWPC were also factored in. This resulted in total QPF through Sunday \nnight amounting to less than inch for most areas with notably higher \namounts in portions of northeast PA and the Catskills where \nlocalized amounts will exceed an inch. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure will build into the region by Tuesday and bring a \nstretch of quieter conditions. Some guidance show some light showers \nkicking off behind the departing low as weak waves move through. \nThere is uncertainty of wraparound moisture will even extend that \nfar west as drier air will simultaneously move in from the east. For \nnow, this updated leaned toward the majority of guidance and NBM \nwith dry conditions for Tuesday. Weak ridging, peaks of sunshine, \nand southwest to westerly flow will help bump up the temperatures \nmidweek with daytime highs in the 70s. \n\nThese pleasant conditions will only last a few days as rain looks to \nreturn late in the week. While models differ on the timing and set \nup, a boundary will extend from the Midwest into the Northeast with \nshowers along it. Where this boundary sets up will determine the \ncoverage of showers and if conditions dry out in time to start next \nweekend. Given the uncertainty, this forecast will maintain low \nchance PoPs late in the week.",
